statement:
Each sentence here is an array of words. You get two sentences, `sentence1` and `sentence2`, and a list `similarPairs` in which every entry `[xi, yi]` declares the words `xi` and `yi` to be similar.

Similarity follows three rules:

- every word is similar to itself;
- it is symmetric: `[xi, yi]` also makes `yi` similar to `xi`;
- it is transitive: if `a` is similar to `b` and `b` is similar to `c`, then `a` is similar to `c`.

Return `true` when both sentences contain the same number of words and, at every index `i`, `sentence1[i]` is similar to `sentence2[i]`. Otherwise return `false`. Words are compared exactly, so upper and lower case letters differ.

validator:
function validate([sentence1, sentence2, similarPairs]) {
  const isWord = (w) => typeof w === 'string' && /^[A-Za-z]{1,20}$/.test(w);
  if (sentence1.length < 1 || sentence1.length > 1000 || sentence2.length < 1 || sentence2.length > 1000) return 'Each sentence has 1 to 1000 words.';
  if (!sentence1.every(isWord) || !sentence2.every(isWord)) return 'Sentence words are 1 to 20 English letters.';
  if (similarPairs.length > 2000) return 'similarPairs holds at most 2000 pairs.';
  if (!similarPairs.every(p => Array.isArray(p) && p.length === 2 && p.every(isWord))) return 'Each pair holds two words of 1 to 20 English letters.';
  return null;
}

